1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,920
Legs or stomach suddenly cramping up after a heavy sweat session?

2
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:08,240
If any of these three sound familiar, it's probably not just tight muscles.

3
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:15,200
Heat cramps come from a mix of salt loss, fluid loss, and muscle fatigue, not just muscle tightness on its own.

4
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:21,920
When you sweat heavily, you lose sodium along with water, and that throws off the signals your nerves send to your muscles.

5
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:29,280
Three things make it worse: being a heavy salty sweater, drinking only plain water, and pushing through tired muscles.

6
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:33,280
First, some people are simply heavier, saltier sweaters than others.

7
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:40,560
If you notice white, crusty marks on your skin or clothes after a workout, that's a sign you're losing more sodium than most.

8
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:47,600
Add extra salt to your sports drink, or grab a salty snack during long workouts, instead of relying on plain water alone.

9
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:55,680
Second, drinking large amounts of plain water during exercise dilutes the sodium you have left, which can trigger cramps even faster.

10
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:02,160
Reach for a sports drink with electrolytes, or pair water with a salty snack or a piece of fruit like a banana.

11
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:11,680
Third, ignoring aching, fatigued muscles and continuing to push through your workout raises your cramp risk, since tired muscles lose fine motor control.

12
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:20,640
When a muscle starts to cramp, stop, gently stretch and massage it, then rest in a cool spot and sip an electrolyte drink while you recover.

13
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:27,280
Salt, fluids, and muscle fatigue - manage those three together and heat cramps become far less likely.

14
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:32,000
If cramps keep coming back or last a long time, it's worth checking in with a doctor.
